d "50bbe0a7-1060-5131-91f7-74c3387b1491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187007 #edb/ref 1080 "3.16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187007 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186187007 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "60587763-da89-56a6-852e-d0aa1b200af8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188065 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10435" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188065 #edb/ref 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 3,$ $\\textrm{Var}[Y] = 0.24,$ and $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 0.2.$ Determine $\\textrm{Var}[X-Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ea98738-258e-5cc4-9e04-58b0b4638290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188065 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X \\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] \\pm 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X - Y] = 3 + 0.24 - 2 \\cdot 0.2 = 2.84.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188067 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188067 #edb/ref 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 5,$ $\\textrm{Var}[Y] = 2,$ and $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 3.$ Find $\\textrm{Var}[2X - 5Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188067 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0bc5206c-e880-5016-9653-49ac54af4d2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188067 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2\\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab\\,\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X-5Y] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 5+(-5)^{2}\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =20+50-60 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188068 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188068 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |\n\nFind $\\textrm{Var}[5X+2Y].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188068 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04bff442-bf0b-529b-a8d8-14538a1db48f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188068 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab\\,\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2,\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\textrm{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space $S_X,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.4+3\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =1.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =5.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5.4-{1.8}^{2} \\\\  & =2.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space $S_Y,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =2-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].$ We find $\\textrm{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0\\cdot f(0,0)+0\\cdot 2\\cdot f(0,2)+3\\cdot 0\\cdot f(3,0)+3\\cdot 2\\cdot f(3,2) \\\\  & =0+0+0+6\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =1.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.2-1.8\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[5X+2Y] & ={5}^{2}\\cdot 2.16+{2}^{2}\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 5\\cdot 2\\cdot (-0.6) \\\\  & =54+4-12 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188069 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188069 #edb/ref 1071 "For three random variables $X_1,X_2,$ and $X_3,$ we have the following variances and covariances:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_1] = 1,   \\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[X_2] = 3,   \\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[X_3] = 8   \\qquad\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2] = 2,   \\qquad\n\\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_3] = 4,   \\qquad\n\\textrm{Cov}[X_2,X_3] = -5   \\qquad\n$$\n\nFind $\\textrm{Var}[5X_1 + 2X_2 + 3X_3].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188069 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d849af65-721a-5a47-b8b7-9446e77046f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188069 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $X_1, \\ldots, X_n$ are random variables, and $a_1, \\ldots, a_n$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}\\left[\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} a_i X_i \\right]\n= \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} a_i^2 \\textrm{Var}[X_i] + 2\\sum\\limits_{i <     j} a_i a_j \\textrm{Cov}[X_i,X_j].\n$$\n\nHence, for $n=3$ random variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3}] & ={a}_{1}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+{a}_{2}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+{a}_{3}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{3}] \\\\  & +2{a}_{1}{a}_{2}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]+2{a}_{1}{a}_{3}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{3}]+2{a}_{2}{a}_{3}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[5{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}+3{X}_{3}] & ={5}^{2}\\cdot 1+{2}^{2}\\cdot 3+{3}^{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & +2\\cdot 5\\cdot 2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 5\\cdot 3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =25+12+72+40+120-60 \\\\  & =209.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188357 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188357 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188357 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188357 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188357 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188357 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188357 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188357 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188357 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Variance of a Sum or Difference of Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188357 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188357 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "91379d24-6b6d-5642-8eb8-cbcc8feaf355" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188359 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188359 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188359 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188359 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188359 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188359 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17